        "The code implements exactly the map\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\phi_j(x)=\\#\\{t:w_t=v_j\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Later chapters will replace this simple count map by TF-IDF, low-rank embeddings, neural embeddings, and transformer representations.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 1.8 What makes language mathematically difficult?\n",
        "\n",
        "NLP is difficult not because text cannot be turned into numbers, but because the numerical representation must preserve useful structure.  Several mathematical difficulties appear immediately.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Variable length\n",
        "\n",
        "Texts have different lengths.  One sentence may contain five tokens, while another may contain five hundred.  Many mathematical models expect fixed-dimensional vectors.  Therefore we need maps that handle variable-length inputs:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\phi: V^* \\longrightarrow R^d.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Bag-of-words does this by ignoring order.  Recurrent networks do this by updating hidden states.  Transformers do this by operating on token sequences and producing contextual vectors.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Discreteness\n",
        "\n",
        "Tokens are discrete.  There is no natural meaning to arithmetic such as\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{cat} + \\text{the}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Embedding methods introduce a learned map\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "e: V \\longrightarrow R^k,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "so that tokens become vectors.  Once tokens are embedded, we can use inner products, distances, projections, and optimization.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Context dependence\n",
        "\n",
        "The meaning of a word depends on context.  For example, the word `model` may refer to a mathematical model, a machine learning model, or a physical object.  A fixed word embedding assigns one vector to each token type:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "e(\\text{model}) \\in R^k.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A contextual embedding assigns a vector to a token occurrence inside a sequence:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h_t = h_t(w_1,\\ldots,w_T) \\in R^k.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus the same token can receive different vectors in different sentences.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Combinatorial explosion\n",
        "\n",
        "If the vocabulary has size $d$, then the number of possible sequences of length $T$ is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "|V^T| = d^T.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Even for moderate $d$ and $T$, this number is enormous.  This is why language modeling cannot simply store probabilities for every possible sentence.  A model must share statistical strength across related contexts.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Ambiguity\n",
        "\n",
        "Language is ambiguous at many levels.  A sentence can have multiple possible meanings, labels, or continuations.  Probability is therefore natural.  Instead of predicting one continuation with certainty, a language model estimates\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_\\theta(w_t\\mid w_1,\\ldots,w_{t-1}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Generation then samples from this distribution or chooses tokens according to a decoding rule.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 1.9 The mathematical pipeline of NLP\n",
        "\n",
        "A typical NLP system can be written as the composition\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "V^*\n",
        "\\xrightarrow{\\phi}\n",
        "R^d\n",
        "\\xrightarrow{f_\\theta}\n",
        "Y.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Here:\n",
        "\n",
        "- $V^*$ is the symbolic input space;\n",
        "- $\\phi$ is a representation map;\n",
        "- $R^d$ is a numerical feature or embedding space;\n",
        "- $f_\\theta$ is a learned model;\n",
        "- $Y$ is the output space.\n",
        "\n",
        "For a probabilistic system, the output may be a distribution rather than a single prediction:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "V^*\n",
        "\\xrightarrow{\\phi}\n",
        "R^d\n",
        "\\xrightarrow{p_\\theta}\n",
        "\\Delta(Y),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $\\Delta(Y)$ denotes the set of probability distributions on $Y$.\n",
        "\n",
        "If $Y$ is finite with $K$ classes, then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\Delta(Y)=\\left\\{p\\in R^K: p_j\\geq 0,\\ \\sum_{j=1}^K p_j=1\\right\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This simplex will appear often because classification and next-token prediction both output probability vectors.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 1.10 A first view of next-token prediction\n",
        "\n",
        "Modern large language models are often trained by next-token prediction.  Given a context\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "c_t=(w_1,\\ldots,w_{t-1}),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "the model outputs a probability distribution over the next token:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_\\theta(\\cdot\\mid c_t) \\in \\Delta(V).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "If the true next token is $w_t$, the negative log-likelihood loss is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\ell_t(\\theta) = -\\log p_\\theta(w_t\\mid w_1,\\ldots,w_{t-1}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For a sequence $(w_1,\\ldots,w_T)$, the total loss is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "L(\\theta)\n",
        "=\n",
        "-\\sum_{t=1}^T \\log p_\\theta(w_t\\mid w_1,\\ldots,w_{t-1}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For a corpus, we sum this quantity over all training sequences.  This is simply maximum likelihood estimation for a conditional probability model.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "## Statistical interpretation\n",
        "\n",
        "Next-token prediction is not magic.  It is conditional probability estimation on a very large discrete space, using a high-dimensional nonlinear parameterization.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "This perspective connects language models to classical statistics.  The novelty is not that the objective is unfamiliar.  The novelty is the scale of the data, the dimension of the parameter space, and the expressiveness of the model class.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 1.11 Example: sentiment classification as empirical risk minimization\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose we have labeled texts\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "D = \\{(x_i,y_i)\\}_{i=1}^n,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $y_i\\in\\{0,1\\}$.  Choose a representation\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\phi: V^* \\to R^d.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A logistic text classifier has the form\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_\\theta(Y=1\\mid x)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\sigma(\\beta_0 + \\beta^T\\phi(x)),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\sigma(z)=\\frac{1}{1+e^{-z}}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and $\\theta=(\\beta_0,\\beta)$.  The negative log-likelihood is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat R_n(\\theta)\n",
        "=\n",
        "-\n",
        "\\frac{1}{n}\n",
        "\\sum_{i=1}^n\n",
        "\\left[\n",
        " y_i\\log p_\\theta(Y=1\\mid x_i)\n",
        "+\n",
        "(1-y_i)\\log(1-p_\\theta(Y=1\\mid x_i))\n",
        "\\right].\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Training the classifier means solving\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat\\theta\n",
        "\\in\n",
        "\\arg\\min_\\theta \\widehat R_n(\\theta).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This example shows the basic structure of many NLP models:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{text}\n",
        "\\longrightarrow\n",
        "\\text{features}\n",
        "\\longrightarrow\n",
        "\\text{probability}\n",
        "\\longrightarrow\n",
        "\\text{loss}\n",
        "\\longrightarrow\n",
        "\\text{optimization}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The later neural models are more complicated, but they still follow this structure.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## 1.12 Interactive HTML: representation and a linear classifier\n",
        "\n",
        "The following HTML component illustrates the simplest version of the NLP pipeline\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{text}\\longrightarrow \\phi(x)\\longrightarrow \\beta_0+\\beta^T\\phi(x)\\longrightarrow \\sigma(\\beta_0+\\beta^T\\phi(x)).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Here the representation has only two coordinates: the count of a positive token and the count of a negative token.  This is intentionally small so that the geometry is visible.\n",
        "\n",
