        "## 1.13 What this book emphasizes\n",
        "\n",
        "This book is designed for students who already have or are building mathematical maturity.  It therefore emphasizes five principles.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Principle 1: Start from the space\n",
        "\n",
        "Always identify the space of objects being modeled.  Are we working with $V$, $V^T$, $V^*$, $R^d$, a probability simplex, or a space of matrices?\n",
        "\n",
        "### Principle 2: Track dimensions\n",
        "\n",
        "Dimension checking prevents many conceptual and programming errors.  For example, in attention,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "QK^T\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "is meaningful only when the matrix dimensions agree.  Later chapters will carefully track the shapes of query, key, and value matrices.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Principle 3: Separate representation from prediction\n",
        "\n",
        "The representation map $\\phi$ and the prediction function $f_\\theta$ play different roles.  A linear classifier on a powerful representation may outperform a nonlinear classifier on a weak representation.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Principle 4: Interpret probabilities conditionally\n",
        "\n",
        "Language models are conditional probability models.  The conditioning information matters.  The expressions\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(w_t), \\qquad P(w_t\\mid w_{t-1}), \\qquad P(w_t\\mid w_1,\\ldots,w_{t-1})\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "represent different assumptions.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Principle 5: Understand loss functions\n",
        "\n",
        "A model is trained to do what its loss function rewards.  If the loss is next-token cross-entropy, the model is optimized for next-token prediction.  Other capabilities may emerge, but the training objective remains central.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 1.14 Chapter summary\n",
        "\n",
        "Natural language processing begins with symbolic, variable-length sequences and turns them into mathematical objects.  The most important objects in this chapter are:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Concept | Mathematical form |\n",
        "|---|---|\n",
        "| vocabulary | finite set $V$ |\n",
        "| sentence | sequence $(w_1,\\ldots,w_T)\\in V^T$ |\n",
        "| all finite texts | sequence space $V^*$ |\n",
        "| corpus | finite sample $D=\\{x_1,\\ldots,x_n\\}$ |\n",
        "| representation | map $\\phi:V^*\\to R^d$ |\n",
        "| classifier | map $f:V^*\\to C$ |\n",
        "| probabilistic classifier | conditional distribution $p_\\theta(y\\mid x)$ |\n",
        "| language model | distribution $P(w_1,\\ldots,w_T)$ |\n",
        "| next-token model | $p_\\theta(w_t\\mid w_1,\\ldots,w_{t-1})$ |\n",
        "| training | empirical risk minimization |\n",
        "| evaluation | estimation of out-of-sample performance |\n",
        "\n",
        "The central lesson is simple: an NLP system is a mathematical pipeline.  Once the pipeline is written clearly, the role of linear algebra, probability, statistics, optimization, and geometry becomes visible.\n",
        "\n",
